# Design: the emitter, written in form. The page spec/core-operations.md is the authority (entries 102, 112 and 128).

n: 5.18
name: the-emitter
kind: hub
is: The emitter written in form: a module of named definitions that compiles definitions over numbers, with what they call, to one machine-code leaf. Its code answers a number or declines, is held to the walker's outcome by a program, and for an entry is a faster form of the entry's own binding.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.1
name: an-emitter-in-form
kind: decision
is: Entry 128, compile, has no code in the bootstrap; form/compile.aarch64.ck binds it to an emitter written in form, a layer of named definitions. One pass over a recipe's body gathers each node's instructions as bytes and packs them into one code leaf, bound to the recipe or to the entry asked for. It knows numbers: any int leaf, arguments, let, the five operations, tests of lt and eq joined by if, and a call of the recipe itself by self or by name (5.18.2). For any other it emits a byte that pack refuses, so a recipe is compiled whole or stays on the walker.
status: inferred
from: 5.10.4 5.14.19 5.14.15 7.13 7.21
src: 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: The emitter is to be a program in the language (5.10.4), and the walker already gives it what it needs: a node's parts, arithmetic, all and pack. Dividing by 256 is done by reading the bytes of a product, since no operation works on bits.
flip: Per-node hand-back to the walker, as 5.10.4 describes, needs emitted code to call the walker; until then a recipe with one unknown node is not compiled at all.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.18.2
name: code-and-walker-are-held-equal-by-a-program
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. ground/code-or-walker.ck runs twenty-three recipes on the walker and again after asking for their code and holds the outcomes equal: nested lets, large and negative integers, division by zero and of the most negative number, tests with a nothing inside, and three the emitter refuses. The emitter was first written again from one recipe of bare numbers into named definitions and gave byte for byte the same code for seven recipes; then it was extended. Six faults planted in it each turned the program red.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: The program's first version saw none of the planted faults: its helper was a bound entry, which runs on the bindings beneath its own and so never saw the code. The numbers are 64-bit integers only. One machine.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.3
name: bound-says-what-runs-an-entry
kind: decision
is: Entry 102, bound, answers the newest binding of an entry or a recipe as the top of the program sees it, as a node: the entry, its implementation, and whether that runs on its own binding. A program can read what is bound; the emitter takes an entry's recipe from it and compiles a call by the recipe's own name only where that name means the recipe itself, which is so for a binding made in a layer.
status: inferred
from: 5.14.19 5.18.1 7.13
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Without it a program could bind and release but not look, and the emitter had to be handed the recipe again.
flip: It answers the top's view and not the asker's layer. An implementation asking about its own entry gets itself.
limit: With a second entry it answers as that entry's implementation sees the first.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.4
name: code-is-a-faster-form-of-the-binding
kind: decision
is: Entry 112, fast, gives the newest binding of an entry a code leaf as its faster form: tried first on integers wherever that binding runs, the recipe running when it declines. The binding stays where it is, so the other definitions of its layer see the code. It steps aside while something bound from outside sits on a bootstrap operation or on an entry the program defines. Observed on 2026-11-26: a definition of the same layer ran on the code; with a counter on add from outside every addition was seen, and without it the code ran again.
status: inferred
from: 5.18.1 5.18.3 5.17.1 5.14.19 7.21
src: 2.30.7 2.30.6
conf: mid
why: Code bound above a recipe is unseen by everything bound before it, which is most of a program written as a layer of definitions. Code is not another meaning of the entry; it is the same meaning in another form, so it belongs with the binding it is a form of.
flip: A form set on a binding is not undone by a fail. Watching from outside turns every faster form off, not only those that replaced what is watched.
limit: Witnessed in ground/code-or-walker.ck, turned red by two planted faults. Code still calls no recipe but its own.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.18.5
name: definitions-compiled-together
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-26. The emitter compiles an entry with every definition it calls into one leaf behind a table of branches, so the leaf lies wherever it is put. A call in the last position is a jump when nothing has overflowed. Two definitions that call each other, and a loop calling a third, ended as on the walker; that loop ran thirty million rounds in 0.4 s. Beneath a faster form that declined, faster forms are not tried again: a recursion deeper than the machine stack was answered by the walker.
status: observed
src: 2.30.6 2.30.7
limit: Numbers only: nothing that reads or builds a node, no backtracking. Before the rule about declining, such a recursion tried its code again at every step and did not end in two minutes. Two more faults planted in the emitter, a wrong slot and a jump that ignores an overflow, each turned ground/code-or-walker.ck red.
at: 2026-11-26
